** At zero drift (ordering exactly mean demand, as candidate 7 does), a little more ordering per day is a net loss. For product 1, one extra unit a day costs about 175 and adds about 140 in storage, while it saves only about 212 in shortages. The balance evens out at a slightly negative drift, about −2 units a day for product 1. That is exactly candidate 4.\n\n   Estimated product 1 losses (extra ordering + shortages + storage):\n\n   | Candidate | Product 1 loss |\n   |---|---|\n   | 4 | ≈ 2000 |\n   | 7 | ≈ 2130 |\n   | 0 / 2 | ≈ 2400 |\n   | 3 | ≈ 3000 |\n   | 6 | > 4000 |\n\n   Candidate 6 also hits the 208-unit capacity limit and begins losing goods to spoilage. Product 0 gives a similar picture, with 4 and 7 nearly even.\n\n5. **Batching (1 and 5) doesn't pay.** Candidate 1 saves about 364 in daily order fees and candidate 5 about 440. But each batch sits in storage roughly one or two extra nights, which costs about 700 for candidate 1 and over 1300 for candidate 5. On top of that, both order about 1.1× and big deliveries risk exceeding capacity. Both are worse.\n\n6. **Robustness.** Under random demand, candidate 4 stays close to the best balance between shortages and storage. Candidate 7 trails by only about 100–200, which is within the error of these approximations."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 3835, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2304, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 3835}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-19T06:50:02.888Z", "request_id": "req_011CgB9aABdXui5ZZZ9mgtdG"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "even